{
  "rationale": "This is the first iteration, so there is no race telemetry or integration feedback to learn from. The design is a prior-driven baseline. The course must be covered in under 3 s (about 1.5 m/s average), so the robot needs enough speed, and the line must stay within 6 cm of centre. A five-sensor array spaced 1 cm apart, with 12 mm footprints, covers the 25 mm tape with overlap, so the signal stays smooth through bends. Sensors sit 5 cm ahead of the axle to read the line before the wheels reach it, which gives the controller lead time at speed. The sensor weights are antisymmetric and scaled to +/-1 at the outer sensors, which keeps the signal bounded. Mass is kept moderate at 0.6 kg to limit inertia during bends. A 12 cm wheel track gives a reasonable turn response. The body is 18 cm long and 12 cm wide. The off-track limit is 6 cm in every case, because tape half-width plus body/4 stays under 6 cm for all widths in range. I expect a completed run on the development tracks if the controller holds the line, but speed-versus-stability is the main unknown.",
  "geometry": {
    "sensor_positions": [
      {"x": 0.02, "y": 0.05},
      {"x": 0.01, "y": 0.05},
      {"x": 0.0, "y": 0.05},
      {"x": -0.01, "y": 0.05},
      {"x": -0.02, "y": 0.05}
    ],
    "sensor_size": 0.012,
    "wheel_track": 0.12,
    "wheel_radius": 0.03,
    "body_width": 0.12,
    "body_length": 0.18,
    "mass": 0.6
  },
  "intent": "Baseline symmetric five-sensor array placed 5 cm ahead of the axle, with 1 cm spacing and 12 mm footprints so the 25 mm tape is always covered by at least two sensors. The aim is a stable first race that establishes the geometry-to-controller interface and gives telemetry on whether the line is held at speed. Expected result: completion on the oval and s-bend tracks, with rms_error below 0.03 m. The slalom may show larger error from the faster bends. The Integrator should report whether the sensor spacing gives a usable signal and whether the mass and wheel track allow the speed the controller needs.",
  "feedback_request": {
    "questions": [
      "Does the five-sensor spacing of 1 cm give a smooth, monotonic signal across the tape edge, or are there dead zones or jumps?",
      "Is the 5 cm forward sensor offset useful at the base speed you chose, or does it add lag that needs extra derivative action?",
      "Is the sensor weight range (+/-1 at the outer sensors) comfortably within kp limits, or does it force a very low kp?",
      "Does the 12 cm wheel track with 3 cm radius reach the speed needed to finish in under 3 s without line loss?",
      "Which parts of the geometry are a constraint on the controller, and what geometry change would you ask for?"
    ]
  },
  "learning": "Baseline assumptions: the 6 cm off-track limit is fixed by the minimum, so body width matters little for scoring. Sensor offset ahead of the axle and sensor spacing relative to the 25 mm tape are the main geometric levers to test next. If the race shows line loss on sharp bends, I will widen the sensor span to about 3 cm or move the sensors further forward. If the robot is too slow to finish, I will reduce mass and check wheel radius.",
  "_presentation": {
    "entity0": {
      "label": "Baseline 5-sensor geometry",
      "notes": "First-iteration prior, not yet evidence-based: five 12 mm sensors at 1 cm spacing, 5 cm ahead of the axle, 12 cm track, 0.6 kg. No race telemetry or integration feedback exists yet, so this is a stable starting point to test against the first race."
    }
  }
}
